  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 328 AM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nShowers from western KS are continuing to develop as we start off \nour rainy weekend. Throughout the day, 500mb heights will continue \nto fall over our area as the shortwave makes its way east and \nsoutherly flow at the surface will increase as high pressure moves \noff to our NE and low pressure develops to our west. Synoptic scale \nlift could support some isolated thunderstorms while moisture \ntransport could support locally heavy rainfall but storms should \nremain sub-severe as low-level clouds and weak low-level lapse rates \nwill limit instability. These conditions will stick around for the \nweekend as a blocking pattern develops at 500mb with high pressure \nover the Great Lakes is sandwiched between the Nor'easter developing \noff of the Atlantic coast and the mid-level trough over the Pacific \nNW and Western Canada. As a result, we could see rain accumulations \nthis weekend of 0.5 inches along I-70 and increasing amounts of up \nto 1.25 inches for NW MO. Deterministic guidance has showers \nclearing out Sunday afternoon but how quickly showers clear out will \ndepend on how the blocking pattern develops. An upper-level ridge \ntries to build back into our area Sunday but it will have to fight \nthe Nor'easter that looks to set up shop over the North Atlantic \nCoast. As a result, showers could potentially stick around for a \nlittle while longer. \n\nAs long as the ridge can progress east, as models currently suggest, \nwe should dry out briefly Monday before we see the potential for \nmore rain in the middle of next week. Monday night into Tuesday, \nthere is another 500mb trough that deepens along the California \ncoast and a tropical system moving towards the Baja California \nPeninsula. Models show these two low pressure systems coming \ntogether over the SW US sometime between Tuesday and Wednesday. The \nsystems then continues to progress east, bringing strong moisture \ntransport into our area with strong, moist southerly flow from the \nGulf at the surface and the tropical system brings moisture \ntransport aloft, making for a very moist atmospheric profile. As a \nresult, deterministic guidance currently has PWAT values around 2 \ninches over MO for Wednesday which is in the 99th percentile for \nthis time of year so we will continue to monitor how this system \nprogresses over the next couple of days.",
